{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Qqju7pGFxjYJxi6ZVKCMaE","type":"tool_result","content":"to the MOSP is presented in [14], but in [15] there is an allusion that a direct attack via LIP may\nbe unfeasible in practice. This last hypothesis is reinforced by the fact that the problem has been\nproved to be NP-Hard (see [16]).\n\f                  J.C. Becceneri et al. / Computers & Operations Research 31 (2004) 2315 – 2332              2317\n\n\n3. Pre-processing\n\n   It is assumed, without loss of generality, that the input data for an instance of the MOSP is\ngiven by a matrix of patterns (rows) and pieces (columns). The sole information about the patterns\nthat are relevant for the MOSP are the pieces types contained in them. Hence, if the set of all\npiece types is p = {p1 ; : : : ; pm } and the given patterns are denoted by P1 ; : : : ; Pv , then, a pattern Pi\nin our problem can be represented solely as a set of piece types {Piu ; : : : ; Pij ; : : : ; Pik }, with Pit ∈ p,\nt = u; : : : ; j; : : : ; k, and the input data can be presented as a v × m matrix of 0’s and 1’s where in\nrow i, corresponding to pattern Pi , there will be 1’s in columns u; : : : ; j; : : : ; k in correspondence to\nthe piece types Piu ; : : : ; Pij ; : : : ; Pik present in pattern Pi , and 0’s in the remaining columns.\n   The input data for an instance of the MOSP can also be given by a graph G(V; E). In G(V; E)\n(from now on denoted simply as G), node k ∈ V represents the piece type pk and two nodes i\nand j are adjacent (that is, (i; j) ∈ E) if and only if the corresponding piece types pi and pj are\nsimultaneously present in some pattern. A pattern with h diFerent piece types de<nes a clique of\nsize h (Kh ) and a feasible solution to the MOSP is associated to a sequence of arcs of G. Moreover,\nan optimal solution to the problem can also be viewed as a succession of (probably) large cliques.\n   The pre-processing phase has two main objectives, (i) to eliminate redundancies and (ii) to detect\nconditions for which a solution can be found in polynomial time.\n   A redundancy occurs whenever in G, a clique is an induced complete sub-graph of another possibly\nlarger one. This case happens whenever Pj ⊆ Pi , i \u0006= j and we say that pattern Pi dominates pattern\nPj . In such a case, Pj can be removed during an optimal solution search of the problem, since Pj\ncan be inserted immediately after the pattern Pi in an optimal sequence, without increasing the value\nof \u0002. It is clear that when Pj \u0006⊂ Pi no such a dominance can exist, even if |Pi | ¿ |Pj |, Pi and Pj\nshare an associated non-maximal clique of size smaller than |Pj |, i.e. if Ks ⊆ KPi , Ks ⊆ KPj and\n|Pi | ¿ |Pj | ¿ s.\n   After the dominance analysis among patterns, in a subsequent phase, we verify if some induced\nsub-graph of G has trees, simple cycles or 1-trees as components. If this is the case, we apply then\nthe algorithms developed in [1] to generate an optimal ordering for the pieces contained in these\ncomponents. The central idea is to explore the structure of graphs which possess “small” cliques,\ne.g. if G is a polygon then the MOSP is solved in linear time and \u0002 = 2. In general, an optimal\nsolution to the MOSP is given by a sequence of possibly large and overlapping cliques, intertwined\nby elements such as trees. Therefore, it is important to exactly solve the schedule of the latter\ncomponents in polynomial time to incorporate them into the <nal solution. After the pre-processing\nphase, we will work with the minor of G, which will be denoted by Gp onwards.\n\n\n4. The heuristic of Minimal Cost Node\n\n  We present next a Minimal Cost Node heuristic that uses the idea of traversing the arcs in Gp ,\nusing as criterion, the least quantity of arcs to be sequenced in order to close the node.\n  The order in which the arcs are traversed dictates the order in which the patterns are sequenced.\n  Let us denote:\n• V is a set of n nodes (associated to the pieces) of Gp ;\n• E is the set of nodes of Gp ;\n\f2318           J.C. Becceneri et al. / Computers & Operations Research 31 (2004) 2315 – 2332\n\n• \u0015(k) is the degree of the node k ∈ V excluding the arcs incident to k already traversed;\n• SETV is an ordered set of nodes of V yet to be sequenced. The nodes are ordered in a non-\n  decreasing manner according to \u0015. A node k ∈ V is in SETV if and only if \u0015(k) ¿ 1;\n• ARC is an ordered set of arcs of Gp already traversed;\n• OPEN is the set of nodes in V already open (at least one arc incident to this node is in set ARC\n  and not all arcs incident to this node is in the set ARC);\n• s is the number of arcs in ARC (|ARC|);\n• \u0002\u0002 is the maximum number of open stacks generated by the heuristic.\n\n  We introduce in the sequence a pseudo-code of the heuristic of Minimal Cost Node.\n\n  Heuristic of Minimal Cost Node\n  Input: Graph Gp\n  Output: Sequence of patterns to be cut and \u0002’\n\n  Build SETV;\n  OPEN ← ∅;\n  ARC ← ∅;\n  s ← 0;\n  While s \u0006= |E|\n         Begin\n               get the degree \u0015(k) of the <rst node k of SETV.\n               get the <rst two adjacent nodes of SETV (n1 and n2 ) with\n                      \u0015(n1 ) = \u0015(k) and that possess pair-wise the smallest measures P.\n               put n1 and n2 in OPEN (if they are not there);\n               get the arc (n1 ,n2 ) and insert it in ARC;\n               s ← s + 1;\n               update P(n1 ) and P(n2 );\n               If (P(nk ) = 0) // k = 1; 2\n                  Then Delete nk from OPEN\n               search the nodes in OPEN trying to <nd adjacent nodes that\n                  have arcs not yet included in ARC.\n               If there are such arcs\n                  Then Begin\n                             put all of them in ARC;\n                             update s;\n                             update the \u0015(nk ) for all end-nodes of the arcs\n                             inserted in ARC;\n                                      update OPEN;\n                             update SETV;\n                             End;\n         End;\n         With the ordered set ARC generate the sequence of patterns from\n         Gp and G to obtain \u0002’.\n\f                     J.C. Becceneri et al. / Computers & Operations Research 31 (2004) 2315 – 2332            2319\n\nTable 1\nMatrix of patterns × pieces\n\nPattern           Piece\n\n                 p1              p2           p3            p4           p5            p6                p7    p8\n\nP1               1               1            1             0            0             0                 0     0\nP2               1               0            0             1            0             0                 0     0\nP3               1               0            0             0            1             0                 0     0\nP4               1               0            0             0            0             1                 0     0\nP5               1               0            0             0            0             0                 1     0\nP6               1               0            0             0            0             0                 0     1\nP7               0               1            0             0            1             0                 0     0\nP8               0               1            0             0            0             0                 1     0\nP9               0               0            1             0            1             0                 0     0\nP10              0               0            0             1            0             1                 0     0\nP11              0               0            0             1            0             0                 0     1\nP12              0               0            0             0            1             1                 0     0\nP13              0               0            0             0            1             0                 1     0\nP14              0               0            0             0            0             1                 0     1\n\n\n\n\n                                           Fig. 1. Graph Gp for the example.\n\n\n\n  For the sake of clarity we illustrate the heuristic with a numerical example. Consider an instance\nwith initial matrix of patterns (lines) by pieces (columns) given in Table 1.\n  The corresponding MOSP graph (G) is given in Fig. 1. Notice that parallel arcs, without loss of\ngenerality, are treated as a single one, since once the <rst arc is traversed, all the parallel ones can\nbe immediately traversed for both nodes are already open.\n  The main solution steps performed by the Minimal Cost Node heuristic are presented next.\n  There is no dominated pattern in this instance (Gp = G). The program executes seven loops to\n<nish. Next we present just four of the loops.\n  Initial condition:\n\n            \u0015(1)          \u0015(2)        \u0015(3)         \u0015(4)          \u0015(5)     \u0015(6)        \u0015(7)           \u0015(8)\n            7             4           3            3             5        4           3              3\n\f2320                 J.C. Becceneri et al. / Computers & Operations Research 31 (2004) 2315 – 2332\n\n  OPEN= { } and ARC= { }\n  SETV = {3, 4, 7, 8, 2, 6, 5, 1}\n\n                              \u0002\u0002                s                 n1                  n2\n\n                              0                 0                 —                   —\n\nFirst loop:\n\n              \u0015(1)        \u0015(2)        \u0015(3)          \u0015(4)      \u0015(5)        \u0015(6)        \u0015(7)           \u0015(8)\n\n              7           4           3             2         5           4           3              2\n\n  OPEN= {4, 8} and ARC= {(4,8)}\n  SETV = {4, 8, 3, 7, 2, 6, 5, 1}\n\n                              \u0002\u0002                s                  n1                 n2\n\n                              2                 1                  4                  8\n\nSecond loop:\n\n              \u0015(1)        \u0015(2)        \u0015(3)          \u0015(4)      \u0015(5)        \u0015(6)        \u0015(7)           \u0015(8)\n\n              7           4           3             1         5           2           3              1\n\n  OPEN= {4, 6, 8} and ARC= {(4,8), (4,6), (6,8)}\n  SETV = {4, 8, 6, 3, 7, 2, 5, 1}\n\n                              \u0002\u0002                s                  n1                 n2\n\n                              3                 3                  4                  6\n\nThird loop:\n\n              \u0015(1)        \u0015(2)        \u0015(3)          \u0015(4)      \u0015(5)        \u0015(6)        \u0015(7)           \u0015(8)\n\n              4           4           3             0         5           1           3              0\n\n  OPEN= {1, 6} and ARC= {(4,8), (4,6), (6,8), (1,4), (1,6), (1,8)}\n  SETV = {6, 3, 7, 1, 2, 5}\n\n                              \u0002\u0002                s                 n1                  n2\n\n                              4                 6                 4                   1\n\f                  J.C. Becceneri et al. / Computers & Operations Research 31 (2004) 2315 – 2332          2321\n\n\n\n\n                      Fig. 2. Open stacks generated (•) during the processing of the patterns.\n\n\n\n\nFinal loop:\n\n           \u0015(1)        \u0015(2)        \u0015(3)          \u0015(4)        \u0015(5)         \u0015(6)        \u0015(7)        \u0015(8)\n\n           0           0           0             0           0            0           0           0\n\n   OPEN= { } and ARC= {(4,8), (4,6), (6,8), (1,4), (1,6), (1,8), (6,5), (1,5), (1,3), (3,5), (3,2),\n(1,2), (2,5), (1,7), (2,7), (5,7)}\n   SETV= { }\n\n                           \u0002\u0002                s                    n1                   n2\n\n                           4                 16                   1                    7\n\n\n   The sequence of patterns to be cut is dictated according to the ordered set ARC. In [12], the\nprocedure to transform a sequence of arcs in a sequence of patterns is presented. The idea is the\nfollowing: “follow the sequence of the arcs and sequence a pattern Pi when, for the <rst time, all\nthe nodes corresponding to all the piece types in Pi are open”. Hence, the <nal pattern sequence\nobtained for this instance is {P11 , P10 , P14 , P2 , P4 , P6 , P12 , P3 , P9 , P1 , P7 , P5 , P8 , P13 }.\n   The open stacks obtained during the processing of the patterns using this sequence are presented\nin Fig. 2.\n\n\n\n5. The exact method\n\n   In this section, we present a branch-and-bound approach for exactly solving the MOSP. The search\nfor an optimal solution follows a depth <rst strategy with a local dominance scheme as described in\n\f2322               J.C. Becceneri et al. / Computers & Operations Research 31 (2004) 2315 – 2332\n\n[13,14], which generated shortcuts in the enumeration of feasible solutions schema. This dominance\nappears from the observation that, if, in a given level, the sequence of pieces pi ; pj is being already\nconsidered, therefore the minimum number of open stacks found so far cannot be diminished by the\npieces pi ; pj relative order reversal. Additionally, a further improvement was incorporated into the","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9449124f-cac2-4834-81bc-904d40b65451","uuid":"211891cc-a4e8-4e20-976e-3a44b2b92428","timestamp":"2026-09-27T16:01:03.509Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"to the MOSP is presented in [14], but in [15] there is an allusion that a direct attack via LIP may\nbe unfeasible in practice. This last hypothesis is reinforced by the fact that the problem has been\nproved to be NP-Hard (see [16]).\n\f                  J.C. Becceneri et al. / Computers & Operations Research 31 (2004) 2315 – 2332              2317\n\n\n3. Pre-processing\n\n   It is assumed, without loss of generality, that the input data for an instance of the MOSP is\ngiven by a matrix of patterns (rows) and pieces (columns). The sole information about the patterns\nthat are relevant for the MOSP are the pieces types contained in them. Hence, if the set of all\npiece types is p = {p1 ; : : : ; pm } and the given patterns are denoted by P1 ; : : : ; Pv , then, a pattern Pi\nin our problem can be represented solely as a set of piece types {Piu ; : : : ; Pij ; : : : ; Pik }, with Pit ∈ p,\nt = u; : : : ; j; : : : ; k, and the input data can be presented as a v × m matrix of 0’s and 1’s where in\nrow i, corresponding to pattern Pi , there will be 1’s in columns u; : : : ; j; : : : ; k in correspondence to\nthe piece types Piu ; : : : ; Pij ; : : : ; Pik present in pattern Pi , and 0’s in the remaining columns.\n   The input data for an instance of the MOSP can also be given by a graph G(V; E). In G(V; E)\n(from now on denoted simply as G), node k ∈ V represents the piece type pk and two nodes i\nand j are adjacent (that is, (i; j) ∈ E) if and only if the corresponding piece types pi and pj are\nsimultaneously present in some pattern. A pattern with h diFerent piece types de<nes a clique of\nsize h (Kh ) and a feasible solution to the MOSP is associated to a sequence of arcs of G. Moreover,\nan optimal solution to the problem can also be viewed as a succession of (probably) large cliques.\n   The pre-processing phase has two main objectives, (i) to eliminate redundancies and (ii) to detect\nconditions for which a solution can be found in polynomial time.\n   A redundancy occurs whenever in G, a clique is an induced complete sub-graph of another possibly\nlarger one. This case happens whenever Pj ⊆ Pi , i \u0006= j and we say that pattern Pi dominates pattern\nPj . In such a case, Pj can be removed during an optimal solution search of the problem, since Pj\ncan be inserted immediately after the pattern Pi in an optimal sequence, without increasing the value\nof \u0002. It is clear that when Pj \u0006⊂ Pi no such a dominance can exist, even if |Pi | ¿ |Pj |, Pi and Pj\nshare an associated non-maximal clique of size smaller than |Pj |, i.e. if Ks ⊆ KPi , Ks ⊆ KPj and\n|Pi | ¿ |Pj | ¿ s.\n   After the dominance analysis among patterns, in a subsequent phase, we verify if some induced\nsub-graph of G has trees, simple cycles or 1-trees as components. If this is the case, we apply then\nthe algorithms developed in [1] to generate an optimal ordering for the pieces contained in these\ncomponents. The central idea is to explore the structure of graphs which possess “small” cliques,\ne.g. if G is a polygon then the MOSP is solved in linear time and \u0002 = 2. In general, an optimal\nsolution to the MOSP is given by a sequence of possibly large and overlapping cliques, intertwined\nby elements such as trees. Therefore, it is important to exactly solve the schedule of the latter\ncomponents in polynomial time to incorporate them into the <nal solution. After the pre-processing\nphase, we will work with the minor of G, which will be denoted by Gp onwards.\n\n\n4. The heuristic of Minimal Cost Node\n\n  We present next a Minimal Cost Node heuristic that uses the idea of traversing the arcs in Gp ,\nusing as criterion, the least quantity of arcs to be sequenced in order to close the node.\n  The order in which the arcs are traversed dictates the order in which the patterns are sequenced.\n  Let us denote:\n• V is a set of n nodes (associated to the pieces) of Gp ;\n• E is the set of nodes of Gp ;\n\f2318           J.C. Becceneri et al. / Computers & Operations Research 31 (2004) 2315 – 2332\n\n• \u0015(k) is the degree of the node k ∈ V excluding the arcs incident to k already traversed;\n• SETV is an ordered set of nodes of V yet to be sequenced. The nodes are ordered in a non-\n  decreasing manner according to \u0015. A node k ∈ V is in SETV if and only if \u0015(k) ¿ 1;